          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "on which it moves. A smaller reading indicates a smaller force of friction, while a larger reading indicates a larger force of friction. From Activities 6.1 and 6.2, we "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "conclude",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that when the force of friction is smaller, the velocity of the stack of coins decreases more slowly, and it travels a larger distance before coming to rest."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.045,
            0.176,
            0.103,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 1,
              "bbox": [
                0.045,
                0.176,
                0.103,
                0.226
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.103,
            0.186,
            0.312,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think as a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.209,
            0.866,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, conduct a thought experiment. We do a thought experiment when the conditions required for the experiment are difficult to recreate in the real world. Suppose, you find an object and a horizontal floor having such smooth surfaces that the force of friction between them is zero. Imagine, what will happen if you repeat steps 3 and 4 of Activity 6.1 with such an object and a horizontal floor? Will the velocity of the object decrease? Will the object ever come to rest or continue moving forever?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.089,
            0.328,
            0.256,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.088,
            0.356,
            0.187,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.088,
                0.356,
                0.187,
                0.446
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.358,
            0.393,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In ancient times, it was well recognised that a force was required to move a stationary object or to stop a moving object. But was a force"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.092,
            0.451,
            0.413,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "required to keep an object moving with a constant velocity? For ages, it was mistakenly thought that a force was indeed required to maintain an object in such a motion. It was only in the 17th century that "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Galileo Galilei",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " argued through a series of thought experiments that if a body moves along a horizontal plane and all impediments to its motion are removed, it will continue to move indefinitely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.695,
            0.328,
            0.862,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.527,
            0.362,
            0.735,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Isaac Newton",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " used the word ‘inertia’ to describe the tendency of objects to resist change in their state of rest or uniform motion, and used this idea to frame his first"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.739,
            0.352,
            0.855,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.739,
                0.352,
                0.855,
                0.459
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.527,
            0.469,
            0.862,
            0.624
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "law of motion. Along with this, Newton presented two more laws of motion in 1687. The formulation of these three laws of motion was a defining moment in the history of science. The unit of force is named after Newton. Remember that when a unit is named after a person, its full form begins with the small case (newton and not Newton) while its symbol is capitalised (N and not n)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.654,
            0.646,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.4 Newton’s First Law of Motion"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.678,
            0.646,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Newton’s first law of motion",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " can be stated as:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.7,
            0.888,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "An object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues to move with a constant velocity, unless a net force acts upon the object."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.737,
            0.888,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In other words, we can say that if the net force acting on an object is zero, the body cannot begin to move or change its velocity. In such a case, its acceleration is zero."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.794,
            0.305,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.817,
            0.859,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "An object at rest means that it has zero velocity. Remember that constant velocity means that there is no change in the magnitude or the direction of velocity. If this constant velocity is non-zero, the motion is in a straight line in the same direction and the magnitude of velocity remains the same."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.943,
            0.113,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "100"
            }
          ]
        },
